From: "aryehcitron@gmail.com" <aryehcitron@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 00:13:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 4.3.0: a failed call's error on the ingest feed (the
 interaction table's error row, the !Type status, !Error for an error with no
 status, the lossless note), in Failures.md and in kronikol export's ERROR
 span; Merging Parallel Reports says the merge keeps error, attributionSource,
 expiredFrom and each shard's background block; HTTP Tracking Setup addresses
 a failed call's error by its response half, where query http prints it

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J2t3gYVNVyokonFWxDXKhy
---
 Exporting-to-OpenTelemetry.md  |  2 +-
 Generated-Reports.md           |  2 +-
 HTTP-Tracking-Setup.md         |  6 ++++--
 Ingesting-External-Captures.md | 10 +++++++---
 Merging-Parallel-Reports.md    |  5 +++++
 5 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Exporting-to-OpenTelemetry.md b/Exporting-to-OpenTelemetry.md
index 5d4d02a..32f8052 100644
--- a/Exporting-to-OpenTelemetry.md
+++ b/Exporting-to-OpenTelemetry.md
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@ One request/response pair becomes **one span**: the request supplies the start t
 | `kind` | `CLIENT`, or `PRODUCER` when the pair is an event (`MetaType: Event`) |
 | `url.full` | `Uri` |
 | `http.request.method` | `Method`, when it is an HTTP verb |
-| `http.response.status_code` | the numeric `StatusCode`; span status `ERROR` when ≥ 400 (the client-span semconv rule) or when the status is the string form of a failure (`Error`, `Failed`, `Timeout`…) |
+| `http.response.status_code` | the numeric `StatusCode`; span status `ERROR` when ≥ 400 (the client-span semconv rule), when the status is the string form of a failure (`Error`, `Failed`, `Timeout`…), or when it is a call that threw, the exception's type behind a bang (`!HttpRequestException`), whose status message is then the call's `error` (4.3.0+; before, such a span was exported `UNSET` with no message) |
 | `db.system.name` | the reverse of the receiver's category mapping (the full list is below the table); omitted for non-database calls |
 | `peer.service` | `ServiceName` |
 | `kronikol.test.id` / `kronikol.test.name` | `TestId` / `TestName` |
diff --git a/Generated-Reports.md b/Generated-Reports.md
index 297d1af..daab2d9 100644
--- a/Generated-Reports.md
+++ b/Generated-Reports.md
@@ -264,7 +264,7 @@ Everything the sequence diagram knows about a call travels with it in the data f
 | `activityTraceId`, `activitySpanId` | **The W3C ids — the bridge from a report to your OpenTelemetry traces and application logs.** Unlike `traceId`, which is Kronikol's own pair identifier. Consumed by `kronikol query trace`, which follows one id across the whole run and flags an id leaking across scenarios. |
 | `capturedBy` | Which capture path produced the entry: `wire` (a proxy or TCP tap) or `span` (an OpenTelemetry receiver). Matters when capture fidelity is itself the bug. |
 | `attributionSource`, `expiredFrom` | How the call got its scenario (3.17.0): `RequestHeader`, `TestContext`, `Scope`, `GlobalFallback`, `Detached`, `None`, `DocumentOwner` (3.19.0: nothing named a scenario, but the call named a document a scenario had written earlier in the run, and it is attributed to that writer for this one call), `DocumentFlow` (3.20.0: a detached flow had just written a scenario's document and this call came between that write and the flow's next operation on the document, and was held until that operation confirmed it), or `Expired` when it inherited a scenario's identity after that scenario had ended and was moved to the root `background` block, `expiredFrom` naming the scenario. `null` on a capture that predates the mark. |
-| `error` | When the call threw instead of answering (3.18.0): the exception's message chain (the outer message, then each inner one after `Caused by:`). The exception's type is `statusText` behind a bang (`!HttpRequestException`) and `statusCode` is `null`. `null` on the request half and on a call that answered. |
+| `error` | When the call threw instead of answering (3.18.0): the exception's message chain (the outer message, then each inner one after `Caused by:`). The exception's type is `statusText` behind a bang (`!HttpRequestException`) and `statusCode` is `null`. `null` on the request half and on a call that answered. From 4.3.0 an ingested capture's `error` fills it too ([Ingesting-External-Captures](Ingesting-External-Captures)), `kronikol merge` keeps it, and `Failures.md` prints it under the calls table. |
 | `durationMs` | Wall-clock milliseconds for the call, derived from the request and response timestamps — or taken verbatim when the capturer measured it and sent `durationMs` (see [Ingesting-External-Captures](Ingesting-External-Captures)). Repeated on both halves of a pair; `null` when the request went unanswered. |
 | `stepPath` | Which step the call happened under: an index into the scenario's steps, `b`-prefixed for a background step (`b0`, `0`, `1`). `null` before the first step, and whenever attribution could not be trusted — a test doing work on a background thread can enqueue a record after the following step's marker, and Kronikol leaves the field empty and raises a `StepAttributionMismatch` diagnostic rather than guessing. See [Background-Thread-Correlation](Background-Thread-Correlation). |
 
diff --git a/HTTP-Tracking-Setup.md b/HTTP-Tracking-Setup.md
index 8b1800e..c68d4b2 100644
--- a/HTTP-Tracking-Setup.md
+++ b/HTTP-Tracking-Setup.md
@@ -113,8 +113,10 @@ A send that throws (a refused connection, a broken response body, `HttpClient`'s
 `TaskCanceledException`) is logged as a response whose status is the exception's type behind a bang,
 `!HttpRequestException`, with the message chain in `error` (3.18.0); the exception is rethrown untouched.
 The fingerprint the history reads names it (`GET /asyncapi/v1.json !HttpRequestException`), the diagram
-draws the return, and `kronikol query http <report> s3/i0` prints the message under the status. Before
-3.18.0 the request stood alone with no reason, which hid every first attempt a retry loop swallowed.
+draws the return, `kronikol query http <report> s3/i1` prints the message under the status (the address of
+the response half, which holds it: `s3/i0` is the request), and from 4.3.0 `Failures.md` prints it under the
+calls table and `kronikol export` sends the call as an `ERROR` span with it as the message. Before 3.18.0 the
+request stood alone with no reason, which hid every first attempt a retry loop swallowed.
 `FailedSend.Status` and `FailedSend.Describe` are public for any other capturer.
 
 ## Non-HttpClient Dependencies
diff --git a/Ingesting-External-Captures.md b/Ingesting-External-Captures.md
index 2cdfd5a..eaee5a9 100644
--- a/Ingesting-External-Captures.md
+++ b/Ingesting-External-Captures.md
@@ -23,7 +23,8 @@ One line per **request** *or* **response**. The shape is the `httpInteraction` o
 | `method` | | HTTP verb (`GET`, `POST`, …) or any custom label (`Query`, `generate [gemma]`). |
 | `content` | | Body text, already decoded and capped by the capturer. |
 | `headers` | | `[{ "key", "value" }]`. Redact secrets — or rely on `--redact` at ingest. |
-| `statusCode` | | Responses only: `"200"` or a custom label. |
+| `statusCode` | | Responses only: `"200"` or a custom label. A call that threw takes its error's type behind a bang, `"!TypeError"`, as the in-process tracker writes `!HttpRequestException`. |
+| `error` | | A call that threw instead of answering: the error's message chain (the outer message, then each inner one after `Caused by:`). Write it on the **response half**, beside the `!Type` status. It reaches `httpInteractions[].error`, `kronikol query http` and a line under `Failures.md`'s calls table (4.3.0+; ignored before). A response with an `error` and no `statusCode` reads as `!Error`, so the diagram, the digest and the history fingerprint treat it as a failure. Any JSON value is read, a string as it is and anything else as its JSON text, so an error object does not make the line malformed. One on a request half is kept there, with a diagnostic. |
 | `requestResponseId` | | Pairs a request with its response (one arrow). Any string; non-UUIDs are hashed to a stable GUID. |
 | `traceId` | | Groups the hops of one call chain. Defaults to `requestResponseId`. |
 | `timestamp` | | ISO-8601. Used for ordering (the diagram follows timestamp order at ingest) and loop durations. |
@@ -45,14 +46,17 @@ One line per **request** *or* **response**. The shape is the `httpInteraction` o
 > diagram use it, and then find it absent from the data file. See
 > [Generated-Reports](Generated-Reports#interaction-fields).
 >
+> From 4.3.0 the same holds for `error`, which the reader ignored until then (`plans/INGEST_FIDELITY_PLAN.md` S2).
+>
 > The other direction, the in-process store written out through `NdjsonInteractionWriter`
 > (`InteractionRecord.FromLog`), was not: it dropped `durationMs` until 3.27.4 (#94), so a measured
 > duration came back as the timestamp delta, and until 3.29.0 it wrote every diagram marker (a step
 > bar, an assertion note, a custom fragment, the Setup/Action boundary) as an empty request line, which
 > an ingest then listed among the interactions and drew as an arrow with no sender (#93). From 3.29.0
 > a marker is one `kind: "marker"` record per half, and an ingest of a projected store draws the
-> diagram the run drew, byte for byte (measured on the LightBDD example suite: 6 of 6). What the
-> writer still leaves behind is pinned, member by member, in `RequestResponseLogRoundTripTests`.
+> diagram the run drew, byte for byte (measured on the LightBDD example suite: 6 of 6). From 4.3.0 a failed
+> send's `error` is written too. What the writer still leaves behind is pinned, member by member, in
+> `RequestResponseLogRoundTripTests`.
 
 ### User actions (`kind: "ui"`)
 
diff --git a/Merging-Parallel-Reports.md b/Merging-Parallel-Reports.md
index d8944bf..67f3930 100644
--- a/Merging-Parallel-Reports.md
+++ b/Merging-Parallel-Reports.md
@@ -314,6 +314,11 @@ Aggregations cross runner boundaries: `kronikol query values merged.json --path
 `kronikol query interactions merged.json --group-by service,status` cover every runner's scenarios in
 one table — the cross-runner comparison a merged file exists to enable.
 
+From 4.3.0 the merge also keeps each call's `error`, `attributionSource` and `expiredFrom`, and each
+shard's `background` block (the calls of no scenario, the expired ones among them, and the
+`afterScenarioEnd` groups computed from them), which every merged report had lost: the reader read none of
+them, so the merged file wrote nulls for the three and had no background calls at all.
+
 > **Before 3.1.0 that last paragraph was not true, and the file did not say so.** The mergeable writer
 > passed no interactions at all, so a mergeable or merged file had zero `httpInteractions` and zero
 > `annotations`, and `services`, `interactions`, `http`, `body`, `values`, `flow`, `trace`, `annotations`
